Name: move folder to trash

Synonyms: move directory to trash

Type: command

Syntax: move folder <pathname> to trash

Summary:
Moves a <folder> to the system Trash or Recycle Bin.

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Security: disk

Example:
move folder "/Users/me/Desktop/old-project" to trash

Example:
move folder (tFolderPath) to trash
if the result is not empty then
   answer "Could not move folder to trash: " & the result
end if

Parameters:
pathname:
Specifies the name and location of the folder. If you specify a name but
not a location, the engine looks for the folder in the defaultFolder.

The result:
If the folder does not exist or cannot be moved to the trash for any
reason, the result is set to "can't delete that file".

Description:
Use the <move folder to trash> <command> to remove a folder in a way
that allows the user to recover it later. Unlike <delete folder>, this
command does not permanently remove the folder — it moves it to the
platform's Trash or Recycle Bin, from which the user can restore or
permanently delete it at their discretion.

Unlike <delete folder>, <move folder to trash> works on folders that
are not empty, since the entire folder is moved as a unit rather than
its contents being deleted individually.

On macOS, the folder is moved using the system's standard trash
mechanism, which correctly handles folders on external volumes.

On Windows, the folder is sent to the Recycle Bin using the shell's
file operation API.

On Linux, the command first attempts to use `gio trash` if available.
If `gio` is not found, it falls back to the XDG Trash specification,
moving the folder to `~/.local/share/Trash/files/` and writing the
corresponding `.trashinfo` metadata file.

References: move file to trash (command), delete folder (command),
revDeleteFolder (command), folder (keyword), command (glossary)

Tags: file system
